<-*-*-*-  Command line  -*-*-*->

<* Summary of arguments>

-h                   displays a short help message
-d                   gives the default values for computing statistics, etc
-cmap filename       colormap
-cc nb               number of color classes
-changetrim          force a change of trimming limits values
+/-line              forces or bypasses the displaying of lines
+/-stat              forces or bypasses the computation of statistics
+/-quant             forces or bypasses the computation of quantiles
filename             file to load

Plus the classical X options.

<* Detail of yap arguments>

-h                   displays a short help message

   A message describing the command line arguments is displayed.

   The program is not run.

-cmap filename       colormap

   By default, the colormap file is searched in the directory defined
   by _CMAP_DIR in the include file yaconfig.h

   If the exact location has to be provided, either by absolute path
   (starting with /) or relative to the current location (starting with ./),
   this path is used.

   Otherwise, the program will look in the directory defined either by:
    - the environmental variable CMAP_DIR
    - the #define _CMAP_DIR in yaconfig.h


-changetrim         force a change of trimming limits values

   The trimming limits are defined in yaconfig.h. When a file is loaded,
   those values are used. However, if one wants to change them, this option
   forces the program to display a window allowing to do it.

   This is only valid in case of a GSLIB format file.

+/-lines            forces or bypasses the display of lines

  Even with the option -line, it is possible to display the lines later
  by switching 'Lines' on in a 2D window.

+/-stat             forces or bypasses the computation of statistics

   -stat can be specified to avoid the computation of the statistics.
   This computation can be long on large files, especially with a lot of
   classes.

+/-quant            forces or bypasses the computation of quantiles

  -quant can be specified to avoid spending time on computing the quantiles.
  If -quant is set, the quartiles will not be computed and it will not be
  possible to define the classes such that each class contains the same number
  of points.

  +stat will be set automatically if +quant is specified

filename            file to load

   This must be a file in a valid format (see File formats for a description).

examples:

   yap -cc 10 +quant -cmap rainbow.cmap test.gsl
   yap -cc 10 -cmap /users/me/yap/mine.cmap TESTS/test.gsl
   yap -cc 10 -cmap ./MYCMAPS/mine.cmap /users/me/TESTS/test.gsl

<* Detail of some X arguments>

-display            X server to contact

-geometry geom      size and position (in pixels)

   geom is of the form:
         wxh+i+j
             where w is the width
                   h    the height
                   i    the position from the left edge
                   j    the position from the upper edge
      or wxh-i-j
             where i is the position from the right edge
                   j    the position from the lower edge

example: yap -geometry 500x400+10+20
 

-bg color           background color

-fg color           foreground color

-iconic             start iconic

<-*-*-*-  File formats  -*-*-*->

<*  overview>

Two main formats with two sub-formats are readable by yap:

- GSLIB (ascii)
  - standard GSLIB
  - "extended" GSLIB
- BINARY
  - FLOAT BINARY
  - CHAR BINARY

The point of the BINARY format is to speed up the reading of the file.
The BINARY formats are only recognized by yap.
A file in BINARY format contains only one variable, whereas the GSLIB format
may contain several variables.

If the input file contains only integer values between -127 and 128, the
CHAR BINARY format allows more compact files.

In case of continuous variable, it is necessary to keep the FLOAT BINARY
or GSLIB format.

<*  GSLIB format>

A GSLIB file is an text (ASCII) file is defined as follows:

    Title
    Number n of variables
    Name of variable 1
    ...
    Name of variable n
    value_1 ... value_n
    ...
    value_1 ... value_n

<*  "extended" GSLIB format>

This format follows exactly the GSLIB format with the addition of the
grid definition.

The second line of the file now contains:

   nb_var nx ny nz

<*  BINARY format>

The BINARY format is only recognized by YAP.

  It contains the description of the grid (number of nodes)
and the minimum and maximum values in the file.

  Two formats are available depending on the type of values in the input file:

- FLOAT BINARY: the values are stored as float. The BINARY file is big but
  faster to load than a GSLIB format file.
  This format is typically used with continuous variables.

- CHAR BINARY:  the values are stored as character. The file is smaller.
  This format is typically used with categorical variables (facies for example).
  The values have to be between -128 and 127

<*  conversion between formats>

The conversion can be done inside yap. Once the file is loaded, chose
"Save as" in the "File" menu to save it in an other format.

The conversion can also be done outside of yap with the two programs:

- gsl2fbin: from GSLIB to FLOAT BINARY
  compiled by: make gsl2fbin

- gsl2cbin: from GSLIB to CHAR BINARY
  compiled by: make gsl2cbin

  Warning: if the values in the GSLIB file are not integer, they are
           rounded to the nearest integer.

  Typically, the values to use for a facies description range
  from 0 to nb_facies-1 or
       1 to nb_facies

See also: Save as in File menu

<*  colormap format>

A colormap file is defined as follows:

    Number n of classes (excluding the extremes)
    r g b for values larger than the maximum
    r g b for class 1
    ...
    r g b for class n
    r g b for values lower than the minimum

The r, g and b values are real numbers between 0 and 1.

Definition of some colors:
0 0 0       black
1 1 1       white
1 0 0       red
0 1 0       green
0 0 1       blue
1 1 0       yellow
0 1 1       cyan
1 0 1       magenta

<!>

<-*-*-*-*-*   MENUS  *-*-*-*-*->

<---------  File menu  -------->

<*  Open>
--------------------------- Open ---------------------------

Loading a GSLIB or BINARY file.

The format is recognized automatically.

Browse through the directories and select the file.


See also: GSLIB and BINARY formats

<*  Save as>
-------------------------- Save as -------------------------

Saving a file in GSLIB or BINARY format.
The output file contains only one variable (the one currently loaded).


GSLIB format: One variable is saved in gslib format (text)

BINARY float format: no information is lost from the original values

BINARY char format:  the values are rounded to the nearest integer, the
                     values are between -128 and 127

BINARY char format (class): each class is saved as an integer value.


See also: GSLIB and BINARY formats

<*  Load colormap>
----------------------- Load colormap ----------------------

Loading a new colormap.

The program will first look if the environmental variable CMAP_DIR is
defined, then _CMAP_DIR defined in yaconfig.h, if none is defined,
it will search in the current directory.

See also: colormap format

<*  Default colormap>
---------------------- Default colormap ----------------------

Loads the default colormap defined in yaconfig.h (_CMAP_DEF).
If this value is not defined, a black to white grayscale is built.

See also: colormap format

<*  Quit>
--------------------------- Quit ---------------------------

Exits the program.

<---------  Edit menu  -------->

<*  Information window>
--------------------------- Quit ---------------------------

Gives some information about what the program does.

<*  Number of classes, min/max>
----------------- Number of classes, min/max ---------------

To change the number of classes and set the minimum and maximum
values to display.

Select the type of scale (arithmetic, logarithmic or by quantiles)

The last option is only available if the quantile computation has been
activated.
The classes are then computed such that each class contains the same number
of points.

<*  Cutoffs values>
----------------------- Cutoffs values ---------------------

To change individually the cutoff values.

The selection is accepted only if the ranking is correct.

<*  Change all cutoffs>
--------------------- Change all cutoffs -------------------

To change the set of all cutoffs at once.

This may not be very convenient with a large number of cutoffs.

The selection is accepted only if the ranking is correct.

<---------  2d slices  -------->

<*  X, Y or Z = cst>

Opens a window and displays a slice in the choosen plane.

The selections are active only if a file has been loaded and if
the corresponding window is not already open.

By default, the grid blocks have the same dimension.

Close:   closes the 2D window
Refresh: refreshes the 2D window
Scaling: allows to display rectangular cells (by specifying a ratio between
         the axis)
Print:   sends the current slice to a postscript file
Stat:    displays the histogram of the slice
Compute: shows basic statistics of the slice

<------------  Help  ---------->

<*  Help>

Index of the help file.

The variable _HELP_FILE has to be defined in yaconfig.h.

<*  About>

About YAP...

<!>

<-*-*-*-*-  Statistics  -*-*-*-*->

<*  Statistics in the main (3D) window>

The "Statistics" button opens a window with the histogram corresponding
to the whole 3D grid.

It is possible to select either cdf (cumulative distribution function
                             or pdf (probability distribution function)

<*  Statistics in the 2D windows>

The same information type of window can be opened using the "Stat" button
in the corresponding 2D window.

<-*-*-*-*-  Compute  -*-*-*-*->

<*  In the main window>

The "Compute" button open a text window which gives statistical information
about the 3D grid.

Each time the "Compute" button is pressed, the information is added in the
same window.

The text can then be sent in an ascii file with the "Dump" button.

If the file currently loaded is name, the text file containing this
information will be called name.stat.

<*  In the main window>

Similarly, the button "Comp" in the 2D windows sends the statistical
information about the slice in the same window.

<!>

<-*-*-*-*-  Printing  -*-*-*-*->

<*  Destination>

Nothing is sent directly to the printer.

Instead, a postscript file is created.

By default, this file is in the current directory.

<*  Options>

By clicking on the "Print" button of a 2D window, a new window appears
to allow to define your postscript output.

The definitions of the offsets and sizes are given in the following figure.

            ________________________________
            |                              |
-           |       Main title             |
|           |   _________________  _       |
            |   |               | | |      |
y           |  T|               | | |      |
s           |  i|               | | |      |
i           |  t|               | | |      |
z           |  l|               | | |      |
e           |  e|               | | |      |
            |   |               | | |      |
|           |  y|               | | |      |
|           |   |_______________| |_|      |
-  y_offset-|       Title x                |
            |______________________________|
               |
             x_offset

               |------- x_size -----|

Giving a x or y size of 0 will generate a file compatible with
GSLIB programs plot1 to plot8. In this case, the postscript file
does not contain the word "showpage" and therefore cannot be sent
directly to a printer.

<!>

<*  Important information>

When a file has to be written (by "Save as" or "Print" or "Dump"), if
a file with the same name exists, it is overwritten.

<!>

<*  Bugs and improvements>

Yes, you'll find bugs.

Some errors can be avoided by making sure the data file are correct,
for example.

Try to follow the information in the "Information window",
it can give clues.

If you find bugs, I would be happy to hear about them to try to correct the
problem.

Also, if you have suggestions to improve the program, feel free to
contact me (my address is in About).

<END>
